34. Qxa4 $6 { The a-pawn is not important. White should have pushed his passed pawn immediately. } ( 34. c6 $1 { ± was the correct move. } ) 
e3 35. Bb4 ( 35. Bc3 $1 { ⩲ } ) 
e2 36. Qd7 Nxh2 $2 { From here the knight is really out of play. It was better to play } ( 36... Rf7 $1 { with unclear play. For example: } 37. Qd3 { Probably } ( 36... Nxh2 36. Qd2 { is better, but the position is basically messy with no clear edge for either player. Now Black is on the back foot. } ) 
Qxd3 38. cxd3 Bf3 39. Bc3 Bxc3 40. bxc3 Nxh2 $13 { and Black is no worse. } ) 
37. c6 $1 Rf4 ( 37... Rf7 38. Qe6 $1 { with the threats of Qxe2 and Rxg2 is very good for White. For example: } Bf1 39. Bc3 $1 { and White wins. } ) 
38. Bc3 $5 { This also wins, but White had the chance to decide the game directly with } ( 38. Rxg2 $1 Qxg2 39. c7 { when Black is helpless. For example: } Rxb4 40. Qf5 $1 { and White ends a queen up. } ) 
Rf7 39. Bxg7 $2 { White had a few winning combinations on hand, but this is not one of them. The best move was } ( 39. Qe6 $1 { which simply wins. There is no need for variations. But there was also something like } ) 
 ( 39. Rxg2 $5 Bxc3 40. Qe6 $1 { and White is winning. For example: } Bg7 41. Qg6 Kh8 42. Rgxe2 { and everything is clear. } ) 
Rxd7 $6 { A minor flaw, but quite interestingly this is again the “obvious” move. Black had a fantastic option in } ( 39... Qe3 $3 { The fabulous idea is that after } 40. Kb1 $6 { For that reason, he should play } ( 39... Rxd7 39. Qd2 $1 Qxd2 41. Kxd2 Nf3 42. Kxe2 Nxg1 43. Rxg1 Rxg7 44. b4 Re7 45. Kd2 Bxc6 { and although it is White who has to play energetically to make the draw, I think that will be the outcome. } ) 
 { Then } Qxg1 $3 { is a brilliant resource. There are variations, but basically White is in trouble. } ) 
40. cxd7 { Finally, with probably less than a minute on the clock, we have arrived at a moment of comparison. Should the black queen give a check on e3 or on f4? } Qe3+ $2 { Wrong! } ( 40... Qf4 $1 { was the correct answer! After } 41. Kb1 Qd6 42. Rxe2 { Black has the saving move } Nf3 $3 { when the computer thinks White’s best chance is to enter a non-standard ending after } 43. Rc1 $5 { though I would be less than happy to have two rooks against queen and knight. The idea that the white pawns will be able to advance without inviting all kinds of tactics is mysterious. Instead, I would probably look at something safer like } ( 42... Qe3+ 42. Rexg2 Nxg1 44. Rxg1 Qxd7 45. Bxh6 $5 { giving up the bishop for Black’s last pawn with the idea of establishing a fortress on the queenside. For example, by playing a3 and Rc1, waiting for the right moment to play c4 and Rc3. Even without the a- and c-pawns, this is a dead draw. } ) 
