 { Armanda – Kozul, Sibenik 2011 } { _ } 24. Rf1+ $1 { This is a slightly surprising check, but it forces the knight to f6. White is obviously better, but the conclusion is very hard to spot. In the game, White’s advantage gradually evaporated: } ( 24. Rad1 $2 { ± } Nf6 25. Rf7 Ke8 26. Rxg7 Bb6 27. Kh1 Ne4 28. Re7 Kf8 29. Rf1 Nf2 30. Rxf2 $5 { Stronger was } ( 30. Kg1 Bc5 31. Rxb7 Ke8 32. Bh4 $14 { but already White’s advantage is not as much as it was and with a downward trend, the practical decision of taking the draw was the right one by the lower rated player. } ) 
Bxf2 31. Rf7 Ke8 32. Re7 Kf8 33. Rf7 Ke8 34. Re7 { 1/2-1/2 } ) 
Nf6 ( 24... Kg8 { is the most natural reply, but White wins instantly with } 25. Rxc7 $1 Rxc7 26. Bd8 $3 { and Black will have to return the rook. } ) 
25. Rf7+ Kg8 ( 25... Ke8 26. Rxg7 { is obviously hopeless to me, but I will add a few moves just to make it clear to the pedants: } Bb6 27. Kh1 Nd5 28. Rd7 { and now, for example, } Bd8 29. Bxd8 Rxd8 30. Rxd8 Kxd8 31. Rd1 { with an easy win. } ) 
26. R1xf6 $1 gxf6 27. Bh6 { The threat is Rg7+ and so on – a typical windmill. } Bb6+ { This is the "best" defence. } 28. Kf1 Rd8 29. Rg7+ Kf8 30. e7+ Ke8 31. exd8=Q+ Bxd8 32. Rxb7 { White wins. } 
